  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-130880",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of students having the flu next week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=25$ students in the class\n\n- the probability of selecting a student having the flu next week is $0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(25,0.3).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(1-0.3)^{25-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{25-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that two or more students from the class will have the flu next week. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=25)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{0})(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{25-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25}+25(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{24}] \\\\  & =1-0.001\\,571 \\\\  & ≈0.998\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
